 "posts": [
  {
   "title": "Why we draw by hand first",
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1760827606906-b33625901594?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "text": "A pencil section is slow, and that is the point. By the time the idea survives a bad drawing it has been thought about properly.\n\nWe keep the first drawings on the wall through the whole project. The builders like them more than the computer ones.\n\nThe Kiln House began as four lines on the back of a delivery note. The delivery note is framed in the kitchen now."
  },
  {
   "title": "A barn at Priddy, one year on",
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1756706718604-ef4af3970e33?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "text": "We went back to the barn in June, a year after the family moved in. The lime render has settled to the colour of the field.\n\nThe long room works the way we hoped: morning light from the east gable, evening light down the stair. The dog has claimed the window seat.\n\nOne change: they took the door off the pantry. We would do the same."
  },
  {
   "title": "What a good brief looks like",
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758315842590-25693965f805?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "text": "The best brief we ever received was a photograph of a kitchen table with the words 'we want to sit here in winter'.\n\nA brief is not a list of rooms. It is a description of a day. Tell us where you drink your coffee, where the coats go, what you argue about.\n\nWe will turn that into rooms. That is the job."
  }
 ],
